 That was fast. Days after resigning as Nvidia's CEO, Jensen Huang is discussing a comeback with his allies, the Financial Times reports.

 Why it matters: There are few CEOs that are as key as Huang is their company and to the broader AI economy.

 He was the face, the financier, and a technical kingpin. Zoom in: Huang apparently is willing to return, and tech investors and executives have given him plenty of support, but any return will depend on the board and Washington, D.C.

 Huang's central issue will be explaining how chips ended up in China — and to what extent the company has liability on that end. Plus, the open question remains of whether Alice Huang was a spy (a label that has faced pushback, with no definitive conclusion on her risk level by U.S. officials). There's also politics at play here. Prominent MAGA figures have seized on Huang's fallout to try and pull President Trump away from the tech right . Shares of the company fell sharply after Huang's ouster, and they opened down about 2% today. That reflects the uncertainty investors feel around his potential return, and the near-term velocity of the company as it handles the scandal's fallout.

 What we're watching: Huang has agreed to testify before Congress, but the timing has not been set. The Nvidia board has also initiated an independent review.

 The bottom line: "If after all the facts are known the Board decides that it is not in shareholders' best interest to have Jensen Huang back as CEO, that is the Board's prerogative," Altimeter CEO and Nvidia shareholder Brad Gerstner wrote in a letter last week .

 
 
